  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & =\\frac{3-3}{3(3)-\\sqrt{27(3)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{3x-\\sqrt{27x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x-\\sqrt{27x})(3x+\\sqrt{27x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{(3x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{27x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9{x}^{2}-27x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x(x-3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(3x+\\sqrt{27x})}{9x\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{27x}}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)+\\sqrt{27(3)}}{9(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
